course: physics
lesson: 38
title: Rockets, record speeds and motion everywhere
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Every push comes with a push back: a rocket throws hot gas downwards very fast, and the gas pushes the rocket up, so it needs no air to push against and works even better in empty space.
explain: You can feel the same push back when a garden hose wriggles as the water rushes out, and a squid uses it to shoot backwards by squirting water from a tube.
explain: To stay in orbit a rocket must go about 28,000 kilometres an hour sideways, so fast that it keeps falling round the Earth instead of back down to it.
explain: Even sitting still on your chair you are flying: the Earth spins and races round the Sun at about 100,000 kilometres an hour, and you do not notice because everything moves with you.
explain: Where there is little friction, motion lasts: a curling stone or a skateboard on a smooth path rolls on and on after one push.
fact: A rocket throws hot gas out downwards and the gas pushes the rocket up; it does not need air to push against.
fact: Rockets work best in space, where there is no air to slow them.
fact: To circle the Earth, a rocket must move at about 28,000 kilometres an hour sideways; at that speed it falls round the Earth instead of back down.
fact: Most of a rocket is fuel, and its stages drop away as they empty.
fact: At launch the rocket speeds up so fast that astronauts are pressed back into their seats, which is why they lie on their backs.
fact: A hose pushes back when water rushes out of it, so firefighters must hold big hoses together.
fact: Squid, octopuses and jellyfish move by pushing out jets of water.
fact: The fastest humans run about 40 kilometres an hour at top speed; a horse runs faster, and a diving peregrine falcon is faster than a racing car.
fact: The Earth goes round the Sun at about 100,000 kilometres an hour, carrying you with it.
fact: The Earth also spins, and the ground near the equator spins round fastest, so a rocket launched there gets a free sideways start.
fact: In a braking car a helium balloon on a string leans backwards, because the heavier air sloshes forward and pushes the light balloon away, as water pushes a cork up.
fact: A curling stone slides far because smooth ice has very little friction, and the sweepers smooth and warm the ice to make it glide further.
fact: Skateboard wheels and bearings have little friction, so a smooth path keeps you rolling, but small wheels catch on gravel, and wrist guards and helmets protect against the falls.
exercise: What does a rocket push against in space? | Nothing outside it; it pushes its own hot gas out and the gas pushes back | The air in space | The Earth far below | The stars nearby
exercise: Where do rockets work best? | In space, with no air to slow them | Only in thick air close to the ground | Only under water, like a submarine does | Only in very cold and frosty weather
exercise: About how fast must a rocket go sideways to orbit the Earth? | About 28,000 kilometres an hour | About 280 kilometres an hour, like a fast train | About 2,800 kilometres an hour at most | About 28 kilometres an hour, like a bike
exercise: Why does a spacecraft in orbit not fall back down? | It is going so fast sideways that it falls round the Earth | There is no gravity at all that high up in space, so it floats | It is held up by a long cable | Its engines push it upwards all the time
exercise: Why do rockets have stages? | Most of a rocket is fuel, and empty parts drop away | Stages let the astronauts change rockets on the way up | Each stage carries a different astronaut | Stages are spare in case one breaks
exercise: Why do astronauts lie on their backs at launch? | The rocket speeds up so fast that they are pressed into their seats | It is more comfortable for sleeping | They need to see the ground | It makes the rocket lighter
exercise: Why does a garden hose wriggle when the water is turned on hard? | The water rushing out pushes the hose back | The water inside is far too heavy for the hose | The hose is trying to curl back into a coil | The tap is shaking the hose from the other end
exercise: Why does it take several firefighters to hold a big hose? | The push back of the rushing water is very strong | The hose is very heavy even when there is no water in it | They take turns to rest | The water is hot
exercise: How does a squid swim fast? | It squirts water out of a tube and is pushed the other way | It flaps its fins like a fish | It walks along the sea floor | It is carried only by the waves
exercise: About how fast do the fastest humans run at their top speed? | About 40 kilometres an hour | About 10 kilometres an hour | About 100 kilometres an hour | About 200 kilometres an hour
exercise: Which is the fastest of these? | A diving peregrine falcon | A running horse | The fastest human | A cheetah at a gentle trot
exercise: About how fast does the Earth go round the Sun? | About 100,000 kilometres an hour | About 100 kilometres an hour, like a car | About 1,000 kilometres an hour, like a jet plane | It does not move round the Sun at all
exercise: Why don't we notice the Earth racing through space? | Everything on Earth moves with it, smoothly | The Earth is moving too slowly to notice | The air is thick enough to stop us feeling it | We only notice it at night
exercise: Which way does a helium balloon on a string lean when a car brakes? | Backwards | Forwards | Straight up without moving | Sideways
exercise: Why do curling players sweep the ice? | To smooth and warm it, so the stone glides further | To clean it for the cameras | To slow the stone down | To make the ice colder and rougher so the stone grips
exercise: Why do skateboarders wear wrist guards? | Small wheels can catch, and wrists take the falls | They help the board go faster by keeping the arms stiff | They keep the hands warm | They make turning easier
test: lol my mate says rockets can't work in space cos there's no air to push on. is he right? | No, the rocket pushes its own gas out and the gas pushes back | Yes, rockets stop once they leave the air | Yes, they glide in space with no engines ever | No, space is full of air
test: In a cartoon, a rocket orbits the Earth slowly, just faster than a car. What's wrong? | It would need to go about 28,000 kilometres an hour or it would fall | Nothing, rockets orbit slowly | It would float away from the Earth | It would orbit even if it stopped
test: Grandpa's sitting in his armchair "doing nothing". How fast is he really moving through space? | About 100,000 kilometres an hour round the Sun | Not at all, he's sitting perfectly still in his chair | About 40 kilometres an hour | About 900 kilometres an hour
test: If you stood on a skateboard and threw a heavy ball forward, then switched to throwing it backward, what would change? | You would roll the opposite way each time | You would roll forward both times, whichever way you threw | You would stay still both times | You would roll sideways
test: shut up and hold the hose properly, it's flapping everywhere!! why does it even do that | The water rushing out pushes the hose back | The hose is alive | The tap is broken | The wind is blowing it about because it is so light
test: In the car, Mum brakes; your backpack slides forward but your helium balloon leans back. Why the difference? | The air sloshes forward and pushes the light balloon back | The balloon is scared of the windscreen | The balloon is heavier than the backpack, so it moves less | Balloons always move the opposite way to everything
test: What would a curling stone do on rough, bumpy ice? | Stop very soon | Slide even further | Fly up into the air | Spin faster and faster
test: Why might a satellite need to be launched from near the equator, where the ground spins fastest? | The Earth's spin gives it a free sideways start | The air is thinner there | Gravity is switched off at the equator, so lifting is easy | The Moon helps pull it up
test: Lila says an octopus swims just like a rocket. Is she right? | Yes, it pushes water out one way and moves the other way | No, octopuses only crawl | No, rockets push on the air, but octopuses push on the sand | Yes, octopuses burn fuel
test: A rocket is mostly fuel. What happens to the empty fuel tanks? | Stages drop away as they empty | They are kept to the end | They turn into new fuel | The astronauts throw them out of the window
test: Which needs the biggest push back to manage: a garden hose, a fire hose or a drinking straw? | A fire hose | A garden hose | A drinking straw | All exactly the same
test: You push your skateboard once on a smooth path and it rolls for ages; on gravel it stops at once. Why? | On gravel the small wheels catch on stones and friction grows | Gravel pulls the board down with magnets | The smooth path pushes the board along | Gravel is always uphill
exam: Identify the principle by which a rocket is propelled in the absence of an atmosphere. | Gas expelled in one direction exerts an equal push upon the rocket in the opposite direction | The rocket pushes against the surrounding air | The rocket is drawn upward by the gravity of the Moon | The rocket is borne aloft by the solar wind
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a satellite in orbit does not descend to the Earth. | Its sideways velocity is such that it continually falls around the Earth | Gravitation ceases entirely beyond the atmosphere | It is suspended by the pressure of sunlight alone | Its engines operate without any interruption so as to hold it above the Earth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the motion of a person seated at rest upon the Earth? | The person is borne round the Sun at approximately one hundred thousand kilometres per hour | The person is wholly at rest in every respect | The person moves at approximately forty kilometres per hour | The person moves solely when walking
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the propulsion of a squid. | It expels a jet of water and is thereby driven in the opposite direction | It is propelled through the water by the rapid beating of its numerous legs | It is carried exclusively by the currents | It is propelled by the combustion of stored fat
